<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><%- include('../partials/head') %></head>
<body>
<div class="app">
<%- include('../partials/sidebar') %>
<%- include('../partials/topbar') %>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="hint mt-0">
        <strong>One URL in, a list of pages that should link to it out.</strong>
        Give it the page that needs internal links. It crawls the site, finds the pages topically closest to
        that target, and — for each one — locates a phrase <em>already present in that page's own copy</em> that
        can carry the link, with the sentence it sits in.
      </p>
      <p class="hint">
        <strong>Why the anchor rule matters.</strong> A recommendation of the form "link from /blog/x with the
        anchor 'commercial roof repair'" is worthless if that phrase is not on /blog/x — the implementer then has
        to write a new sentence and decide where it goes, and a linking task has quietly become a writing brief.
        So every anchor here is a verbatim substring of the source page's own rendered text. Anchors found only
        in a nav, header, footer or other template region are rejected: a link added there is sitewide, not
        editorial. Generic labels ("Learn More", "Read more") are rejected too — they carry no signal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%- include('_partials/brandpicker') %>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head"><h3>Find links for a URL</h3></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <form method="POST" action="/ai-seo/link-opportunities/run">
        <% if (brand) { %><input type="hidden" name="brand_id" value="<%= brand.id %>"><% } %>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="url">Target URL <span class="muted">(the page that needs links pointing at it)</span></label>
          <input type="url" id="url" name="url" required placeholder="https://example.com/services/flagship-service/">
        </div>

        <div class="field-row">
          <label class="field">
            <span>Crawl up to</span>
            <select name="max_pages">
              <option value="40">40 pages — quick</option>
              <option value="120" selected>120 pages</option>
              <option value="250">250 pages</option>
              <option value="400">400 pages — slow, thorough</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Return at most</span>
            <select name="limit">
              <option value="20">20 rows</option>
              <option value="50" selected>50 rows</option>
              <option value="100">100 rows</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Minimum relevance</span>
            <select name="min_relevance">
              <option value="0.03">0.03 — permissive, more rows</option>
              <option value="0.08" selected>0.08 — balanced</option>
              <option value="0.15">0.15 — strict, only close matches</option>
              <option value="0.25">0.25 — very strict</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <p class="hint mt-0">
          Relevance is entity overlap with the target (weighted 0.65) plus vocabulary similarity (0.35). Entity
          overlap is the selective half: two pages that both discuss "Basel III" and "capital adequacy" are
          genuinely related, whereas two pages that both contain "training" are not.
        </p>

        <div class="field">
          <label for="extra_phrases">Additional anchor phrases
            <span class="muted">(one per line — optional)</span>
          </label>
          <textarea id="extra_phrases" name="extra_phrases" rows="3"
                    placeholder="commercial roof survey&#10;flat roof membrane replacement"></textarea>
          <p class="hint mt-0">
            The target page's own H1, title, slug, H2s and repeated phrases are used automatically. Add anything
            else you would accept as an anchor. A supplied phrase is still only offered where it appears verbatim
            in a source page — supplying it is not licence to invent one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="btn-row">
          <button type="submit" class="btn" <%= perms.canWrite ? '' : 'disabled' %>>Find link opportunities</button>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="/ai-seo/architecture">Whole-site architecture instead</a>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="hint mt-0">
        <strong>How this differs from the two existing linking tools.</strong>
        <a href="/linking">/linking</a> is a full-site crawl producing a spreadsheet of every pair on the site —
        right for an audit, no help when the question is "this page needs links, from where".
        <a href="/ai-seo/architecture">Linking &amp; architecture</a> builds the whole link graph and proposes
        links across all of it, ordered by global overlap, so one page's opportunities are scattered through
        sixty rows about other pages. This is the target-first view of the same data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%- include('_partials/runs') %>

<%- include('../partials/sidebar-end') %>
<%- include('../partials/foot') %>
